WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.630
There's nothing quite like a great science
fiction movie, and, according to Metacritic,

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:08.080
these are the very best of the best.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.309
Here's the question, though: how many have
you seen?

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:14.110
"You've taken your first step into a larger
world."

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:16.109
Star Wars: Perhaps you've heard of it.

00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:21.830
The 1977 original that we now call A New Hope
turned Hollywood completely upside down, becoming

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:26.510
the highest-grossing film of all time and
holding that record until it was dethroned

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.860
by E.T. in 1982.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.979
The initial critical response was mixed, but
the masses who spoke so loudly with their

00:00:32.979 --> 00:00:37.379
wallets have since been vindicated with near-universal
acclaim in hindsight.

00:00:37.379 --> 00:00:40.879
Today, the movie holds a score of 90 on Metacritic.

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:45.899
Star Wars represented a quantum leap forward
in filmmaking technology, dazzling audiences

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:49.510
with visual effects the likes of which they'd
never seen.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:54.989
But neat tricks aren't the only driving force
behind director George Lucas' greatest achievement.

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:59.940
This is a fictional world that feels so real
that viewers can't help but become absorbed

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:00.940
in it.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.430
Certainly, there's no greater example of science
fiction's escapist appeal — or its versatility

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.340
as a genre — than Star Wars.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:12.530
The movie combines elements of war epics,
classic Spaghetti Westerns, and sword-and-sorcery

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:16.429
fantasy into a sci-fi setting that really
does have something for everyone.

00:01:17.819 --> 00:01:23.100
In the future of Mad Max, an ecological disaster
has left Earth bone-dry, and the survivors

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.429
of the apocalypse now battle over the remaining
supplies of water and oil.

00:01:27.429 --> 00:01:32.969
Between the 1979 original Mad Max and its
two sequels in the 1980s, each film in the

00:01:32.969 --> 00:01:37.460
series upped the ante on grim yet stylish
over-the-top action.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:43.179
In 2015, however, director George Miller took
things to a whole new level with Mad Max:

00:01:43.179 --> 00:01:44.179
Fury Road.

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:49.520
The fourth film in the franchise is an intense,
non-stop chase movie in which hard-luck hero

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.030
Max Rockatansky teams up with the devastatingly
cool Imperator Furiosa.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:58.640
Together, they help five women escape slavery
at the hands of a revolting despot.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.659
Fury Road is a top-notch action movie with
eye-popping stunts and practical effects and

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:06.079
award-winning costume and production design.

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:09.619
These qualities alone were enough to earn
it high praise from critics upon release,

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:14.610
of course, but Fury Road has also been held
up as a feminist film, too, tackling themes

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.819
and issues rarely found in action movies.

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:19.220
"You cannot own a human being!

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.989
Sooner or later, someone pushes back!"

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:28.629
The film's combination of brains, compassion,
and pure adrenaline led to the kind of major

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:34.030
awards recognition that genre flicks rarely
receive, too, as well as a near-perfect Metascore

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:35.030
of 90.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:39.700
It now stands as an example of how sci-fi
can educate and empower as well as entertain.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.930
Not every great science fiction film is a
blockbuster hit.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:48.220
Indeed, Russian director Aleksei German's
2013 adaptation of the Strugatsky rothers'

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.400
novel Hard to Be a God grossed just under
$1.3 million worldwide.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.560
It is an opaque and difficult watch even in
the eyes of those who love it.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.120
AV Club critic Ignatiy Vishnevetsky wrote:

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.720
"If Hard to Be a God isn't the filthiest,
most fetid-looking movie ever made, it's certainly

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:05.720
in the top three."

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.890
Meanwhile, The Guardian's Peter Bradshaw asked
in his five-star review:

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.360
"What on earth does it mean?"

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:15.340
But, if the film's Metacritic score of 90
is anything to go by, Hard to Be a God is

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:16.829
a challenge worth accepting.

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:20.690
The plot reads like a cautionary tale out
of a classic Star Trek episode, though the

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:23.099
novel predates the series by a few years.

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:28.349
A team of scientists is dispatched to an Earth-like
planet whose social and technological development

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:33.579
are centuries behind our own, held back by
a stubborn and violent anti-intellectualism.

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:38.000
The protagonists are tasked with anonymously
kick-starting the culture's progress, an effort

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.800
that is repeatedly met with disaster and disappointment.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.569
Do not approach Hard to Be a God expecting
anything conventional.

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:50.780
The Telegraph's Robbie Collin warns that the
film "[refuses] to take any recognizably plot-like

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:56.270
shape," but assures that an open-minded viewer
will find it, quote, "exhilarating, moving,

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.750
funny, beautiful, and unshakeable."

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:04.569
In the early 1930s, Hollywood brought to life
a parade of movie monsters who remain firmly

00:04:04.569 --> 00:04:06.810
in the public consciousness even today.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.610
But one stands taller above all the rest.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:10.709
"Oh, no.

00:04:10.709 --> 00:04:13.060
It wasn't the airplanes.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:16.519
'Twas beauty killed the beast."

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:21.180
King Kong was the first great movie monster
to be created solely for the big screen, rather

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.390
than having been adapted from literature or
mythology.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:28.210
And sure, inventing a really big ape may not
have been the wildest leap of the imagination,

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:32.590
but the story of the 1933 movie, as well as
the technology invented to execute it, has

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:35.950
held the attention of cinephiles for the better
part of a century.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:39.980
Effects supervisor Willis O'Brien brought
King Kong's titular character and his dinosaur

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:44.950
foes to life by employing practically every
special effects trick that had been invented:

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.820
stop motion, matte paintings, rear-screen
projection, miniatures, and giant puppets.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:54.190
And he combined them with live-action photography
in a more seamless manner than had ever been

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.270
seen before.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:59.740
King Kong was met with instant critical and
commercial success, and despite a string of

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:05.729
sequels, remakes, and reboots, the 1933 original
still reigns supreme with its Metascore of 90.

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:09.780
Viewer beware, though, that King Kong was
very much made in 1933.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:15.000
In the decades since, some scholars and critics
have come to see the film as a racist allegory,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.450
and while the film's co-directors denied any
such intent, it's certainly not difficult

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:20.450
to read it that way.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:25.370
Originally released as a trilogy of shorts,
It's Such a Beautiful Day is a mixed-media

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:29.640
film built around pen-and-paper stick figure
animations that, despite their simple forms,

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.180
are remarkably expressive and sympathetic.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.870
The film tells the story of Bill, a man coping
with a brain disease that is gradually destroying

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.500
his memory and eroding his grip on reality.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.520
The story is told in a stream-of-consciousness
style that takes the audience closely into

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.070
Bill's thoughts as he examines his life, his
family history, and the nature of existence.

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:52.830
Remarkably, this animated film is the product
of a single creator, Don Hertzfeldt, from

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:56.800
its hand-drawn character performances to its
narration and sound effects.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.460
It's Such a Beautiful Day is also a brutally
dark comedy, one so dark that it's actually

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.090
hard to call it a comedy at all.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:08.020
Indeed, Vulture's Bilge Ebiri calls it one
of the saddest films he's ever seen, but nevertheless

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.480
ranks it among the best films of the 2010s.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.230
In a 2020 retrospective, Ebiri wrote:

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:18.080
"Hertzfeldt's attention to the fleeting glories
of existence makes you feel one with the universe."

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.280
But along with being profound, It's Such a
Beautiful Day is also a deeply intimate film

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:26.300
that makes you empathize with its crudely
drawn protagonist on a level you might not

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:27.300
think possible.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:33.300
It holds a score of 90 on Metacritic, where
it is also ranked as the 20th greatest animated

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:34.710
film of all time.

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:40.210
In Andrei Tarkovsky's 1972 film Solaris, a
psychologist is sent to investigate a space

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:44.210
station whose entire crew has suffered some
sort of emotional breakdown.

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:48.380
While initially dismissing their reports of
bizarre visions as mere hallucinations, the

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:52.300
psychologist soon finds himself face to face
with his late wife.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:53.860
What is she, really?

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:59.180
Has the strange ocean planet below somehow
reunited them, or is she some sort of illusion?

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:02.430
And even if she isn't real, can't they still
be in love?

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:07.460
Solaris draws easy comparisons to 2001: A
Space Odyssey, another slow, deliberate, and

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.500
visually stunning art film set in space.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:12.520
But, as Roger Ebert wrote in a retrospective
review:

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:17.890
"[2001] is outward, charting man's next step
in the universe, while [Solaris] is inward,

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:21.460
asking about the nature and reality of the
human personality."

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:25.780
The film has an intriguing sci-fi conceit
in its titular sentient planet, which attempts

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:28.770
to communicate to the humans above using their
own memories.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:32.960
But as in most great speculative fiction,
the otherworldly element is just a device

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.310
through which to explore character, and by
extension, the audience.

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:41.780
Solaris premiered at the 1972 Cannes Film
Festival, where it won the Grand Prix Spécial

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.060
du Jury and was nominated for the Palme d'Or.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:49.370
An American remake was produced in 2002, directed
by Steven Soderbergh, but is not as highly

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:52.810
regarded, with a Metascore of 65 to the Tarkovsky
version's 90.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:58.990
In Steven Spielberg's 1977 movie Close Encounters
of the Third Kind, electrician Roy Neary becomes

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:03.180
obsessed with the search for extraterrestrial
life after having a run-in with a low-flying

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:04.180
UFO.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:09.150
Neary discovers that he and a number of other
UFO witnesses have been sharing strange visions,

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.400
leading them on a daring quest for the truth.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.790
"Have you recently had a close encounter?"

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:14.790
"[French]"

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:21.140
"A close encounter with something very unusual?"

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:25.440
While not a marathon of effects-heavy set
pieces like Star Wars, which was released

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.800
that same year, Close Encounters nevertheless
features one of the most arresting finales

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:31.360
in sci-fi history.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.689
Above all, this is an adventure story — one
that's light on action but heavy on suspense,

00:08:36.689 --> 00:08:41.399
intrigue, and wonder, keeping the story grounded
in the point of view of everyday people.

00:08:41.399 --> 00:08:45.410
In a contemporary review of the movie, Gary
Arnold of The Washington Post wrote:

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:50.269
"Spielberg seems to be whispering, 'This is
going to scare you, but don't be afraid.'

00:08:50.269 --> 00:08:55.779
Spielberg puts on an enchanting magic show,
springing one sensuous and sentimental highlight

00:08:55.779 --> 00:08:56.779
after another."

00:08:56.779 --> 00:09:02.250
Authentic performances, groundbreaking effects,
and a gorgeous John Williams score have made

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:07.461
Close Encounters an enduring favorite among
critics, and it now boasts a Metascore of 90.

00:09:07.461 --> 00:09:12.685
The film was nominated for seven Academy Awards,
winning for cinematography and sound effects editing.

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:17.860
In 1980, after seeing Spielberg's revised
Special Edition of the film, Roger Ebert called

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:21.709
it, quote, "one of the great moviegoing experiences."

00:09:22.709 --> 00:09:26.459
Many of the highest-scoring sci-fi films on
Metacritic like to show their audiences something

00:09:26.459 --> 00:09:33.050
grand and awe-inspiring, but Spike Jonze's
2013 drama Her focuses on something much smaller:

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:34.230
your cellphone.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:38.310
Joaquin Phoenix plays a lonely man who falls
in love with Samantha, the self-aware operating

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.430
system on his phone and computer.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:44.600
The film takes a realistic and down-to-earth
approach to its premise, fleshing out the

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:49.630
romance before gradually broadening its scope
to examine the grander implications of bringing

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:52.910
a thinking, feeling artificial intelligence
into the world.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:58.000
Her wrings a lot of humor and pathos out of
its bizarre yet relatable premise, raising

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.480
questions not only about our culture's increasing
dependence on technology, but also on how

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.180
we relate to each other as human beings.

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:07.930
What do we, or should we, look for in love?

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:11.620
How much of love is about the other person,
and how much is selfish?

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:14.420
What happens when one partner needs the other
more?

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:19.310
And all of these questions are centered around
an awkward introvert and the shiny rectangle

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:24.050
he carries in his pocket, brought to life
by terrific, committed performances by Phoenix

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:25.680
and his co-star, Scarlet Johannsson.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:31.110
On top of awarding it a Metascore of 90, Metacritic
found that Her topped 22 different lists of

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.389
critics' favorite films of 2013.

00:10:33.389 --> 00:10:38.199
Her was also nominated for five Academy Awards,
including Best Picture, and brought home the

00:10:38.199 --> 00:10:41.149
prestigious Oscar for Best Original Screenplay.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:47.519
Few sci-fi films hold such a treasured place
in the hearts of '80s kids as Steven Spielberg's

00:10:47.519 --> 00:10:48.519
E.T.

00:10:48.519 --> 00:10:49.820
the Extra-Terrestrial.

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:51.949
Released in the spring of 1982, E.T.

00:10:51.949 --> 00:10:57.120
was a massive success, drawing in families
in such droves that it supplanted Star Wars

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.350
as the highest-grossing film of all time.

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:01.639
Like Spielberg's other sci-fi films, E.T.

00:11:01.639 --> 00:11:06.140
features moments of eerie suspense and exciting
adventure, but it also has something going

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:12.339
for it that most genre blockbusters don't:
It's adorable.

00:11:12.339 --> 00:11:16.170
"E.T. home phone…"

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:17.449
E.T.

00:11:17.449 --> 00:11:21.699
tells the story of Elliot Taylor, played by
10-year-old Henry Thomas, and a benign alien

00:11:21.699 --> 00:11:26.819
visitor represented by an advanced animatronic
and performed by a variety of puppeteers and

00:11:26.819 --> 00:11:28.430
costumed actors.

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:33.769
Made long before CGI became the norm, E.T.
is so emotive and charismatic that it takes

00:11:33.769 --> 00:11:36.870
no time to forget that he's just a special
effect.

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:40.610
And when his time on Earth takes a toll on
E.T.'s health, you'll likely find yourself

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:44.720
genuinely concerned, even anguished with worry
over his well-being.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:47.817
Don't worry — everyone else felt the same
way.

00:11:47.817 --> 00:11:52.580
E.T. was a hit with critics upon its release, and
continues to tug on their heartstrings today.

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:55.999
In 2012, Christy Lemire of The Philadelphia
Inquirer wrote:

00:11:55.999 --> 00:11:59.430
"How old is too old to sob like a little girl
at E.T.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:00.810
the Extra-Terrestrial?

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.399
Not 40, apparently."

00:12:02.399 --> 00:12:04.509
The film holds a Metascore of 91.

00:12:05.509 --> 00:12:11.269
Hailing from Thai director Apichatpong Weerasethakul
, Memoria is perhaps one of the most enigmatic

00:12:11.269 --> 00:12:13.000
sci-fi films of all time.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:18.050
It debuted in July 2021 at the Cannes Film
Festival, where it was awarded the Jury Prize,

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:23.204
one of the event's highest honors — but
its theatrical release has been extremely limited.

00:12:23.204 --> 00:12:27.790
Still, those critics who have seen it have
heaped praise aplenty upon the film, winning

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:29.860
it a Metascore of 91.

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:33.920
Polygon's Robert Daniels warns that the movie
is a little metaphor-heavy, but nevertheless

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:34.920
writes:

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:41.129
"Memoria is a sensory explosion, and its dense,
immersive shrapnel isn’t easily removable."

00:12:41.129 --> 00:12:45.410
Memoria is the story of Jessica, a Scottish
woman living in Colombia, who is haunted by

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:49.999
a thunderous recurring sound that only she
can hear — but this isn't so much a mystery

00:12:49.999 --> 00:12:53.880
as it is a character study and existential
meditation.

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:58.620
All in all, Memoria may not be every sci-fi
enthusiast's cup of tea, of course, but it's

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:03.370
a must-see for fans of art house cinema…
that is, if it's ever playing near you.

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:09.360
Mary Shelley's 1818 novel Frankenstein has
been described as the first true work of science

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:13.649
fiction, a tale of science gone wrong with
a strong moral and emotional core.

00:13:13.649 --> 00:13:18.199
You likely know the story well — Dr. Frankenstein
attempts to conquer death by constructing

00:13:18.199 --> 00:13:23.259
a new man from the bodies of the dead and
reanimating his tissue via advanced chemistry.

00:13:23.259 --> 00:13:27.290
"It's alive, it's alive, oh, it's alive!

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:29.600
It's alive, it's alive!"

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:34.559
His Creature does indeed come to life, but
is shunned by society for his imposing figure

00:13:34.559 --> 00:13:39.050
and his unnatural origins, leading to a tragic
and violent ending.

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:43.470
Nothing has boosted the cultural impact of
Shelley's tale so much as director James Whale's

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:48.639
1931 film adaptation, which redefined the
story and characters of Frankenstein into

00:13:48.639 --> 00:13:51.300
the iconic tale most familiar to the public
today.

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:56.339
Boris Karloff's performance as the shambling,
wordless Creature, as well as his iconic appearance

00:13:56.339 --> 00:14:00.519
by makeup artist Jack Pierce, are now inseparable
from the character.

00:14:00.519 --> 00:14:05.509
Later film adaptations featuring an eloquent,
intelligent Creature now just feel wrong,

00:14:05.509 --> 00:14:08.129
despite being more accurate to the source
material.

00:14:08.129 --> 00:14:11.870
Karloff even went on to reprise the role in
three sequels.

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:14.440
Frankenstein's impact on Hollywood was felt
instantly.

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:18.790
In his review for The New York Times, Mordaunt
Hall called the film, quote, "far and away

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:21.069
the most effective thing of its kind."

00:14:21.069 --> 00:14:26.100
Nearly a century later, critics still consider
Frankenstein one of the pinnacles of both

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:31.360
sci-fi and horror, and it stands tall today
with a Metascore of 91.

00:14:32.559 --> 00:14:37.110
Werckmeister Harmonies is a Hungarian art
film from directors Béla Tarr and Ágnes

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:41.689
Hranitzky, in which the arrival of a bizarre
traveling circus prompts a small town to descend

00:14:41.689 --> 00:14:43.119
into madness and despair.

00:14:43.119 --> 00:14:48.089
The circus is led by a shadowy figure called
The Prince, whose face is never shown but

00:14:48.089 --> 00:14:53.050
whose words propel the townsfolk towards an
unnerving and inevitable revolution.

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:57.119
The story is told from the perspective of
a benign and thoughtful young man who, along

00:14:57.119 --> 00:15:01.009
with his two elderly uncles, becomes the target
of the town's new regime.

00:15:01.009 --> 00:15:06.920
Werckmeister Harmonies is 145 minutes long
and contains only 38 cuts; apparently, if

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:10.829
there weren't physical limitations on how
much film his camera could store at a time,

00:15:10.829 --> 00:15:13.480
Tarr claims he would have made each take even
longer.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:18.389
These unbroken shots embed the audience deeply
into the setting and force them to mingle

00:15:18.389 --> 00:15:22.559
with the characters, even following them around
as they walk silently through the town for

00:15:22.559 --> 00:15:23.730
minutes at a time.

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:26.189
Roger Ebert wrote of this style of filmmaking:

00:15:26.189 --> 00:15:30.600
"Béla Tarr's style seems to be an attempt
to regard his characters with great intensity

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:35.679
and respect, to observe them without jostling
them, to follow unobtrusively as they move

00:15:35.679 --> 00:15:40.499
through their worlds, which look so ordinary
and are so awesome, like ours."

00:15:40.499 --> 00:15:45.410
While seen by relatively few filmgoers, Werckmeister
Harmonies is adored by critics.

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:49.540
In his review, Lawrence Van Gelder of The
New York Times called it, quote, "mysterious,

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.769
poetic and allusive [...] Flowing like a torturous
dream."

00:15:52.769 --> 00:15:56.639
Critical consensus has awarded this film a
Metascore of 92.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:01.709
Science fiction can often be aspirational,
showing audiences a future they'd like to

00:16:01.709 --> 00:16:04.700
live in and even suggesting ways they might
get there.

00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:08.889
It can also serve the exact opposite purpose,
however — warning the public of the dangers

00:16:08.889 --> 00:16:12.990
of their current path and appealing to them
to change course before it's too late.

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:18.850
Threads, a documentary-style film produced
for the BBC in 1984, is an unflinching, unblinking

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:24.730
portrayal of nuclear war designed for one
purpose: to terrify its audience into action.

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:28.710
According to a 2009 interview with The Scotsman,
director Mick Jackson claimed he felt that

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:32.829
the threat of nuclear annihilation was so
enormous that people lacked the vocabulary

00:16:32.829 --> 00:16:34.389
to even comprehend it.

00:16:34.389 --> 00:16:39.209
With Threads, he hoped to turn his extensive
research into something visceral and brutal

00:16:39.209 --> 00:16:42.449
that would help its audience put words and
images to their fear.

00:16:42.449 --> 00:16:47.279
The story extends far beyond the events of
a nuclear bombing itself into the months and

00:16:47.279 --> 00:16:51.600
years that follow, depicting suffering on
an unimaginable scale.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:56.579
Even decades later, Threads makes for one
of the most disturbing movie experiences of

00:16:56.579 --> 00:16:57.579
all time.

00:16:57.579 --> 00:16:59.679
In 2012, The Guardian's Peter Bradshaw wrote:

00:16:59.679 --> 00:17:05.280
"The only film I have been really and truly
scared and indeed horrified by [...] is Mick

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:08.370
Jackson’s post-nuclear apocalypse movie
Threads."

00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:14.140
This visceral look at an alternate past — and
a potential future — scores a 92 rating

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:15.160
on Metacritic.

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:21.250
In the 1950s, alien invasion stories were
all the rage in Hollywood, and for obvious

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:22.250
reason, too.

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:26.570
It was the nuclear age, a time when the wonders
of outer space felt closer than ever — as

00:17:26.570 --> 00:17:30.940
did the threat of conquest by those devious
Soviets.

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:35.430
Extraterrestrials on film became a way to
personify this cultural anxiety or to criticize

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:36.430
it.

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:40.170
Depending on who you ask, the original 1956
version of Invasion of the Body Snatchers

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:41.880
does both… or neither.

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:44.960
"Can't you see, they're after you!

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:47.530
They're after all of us!

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:50.100
Our wives, our children, everyone!

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:51.640
They're here already!"

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:55.530
Invasion of the Body Snatchers is a thriller
following two suburbanites who discover that

00:17:55.530 --> 00:18:00.180
their neighbors are being secretly replaced,
one by one, with identical copies of alien

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:02.570
origin bent on world conquest.

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:06.690
The tension grows as they begin to question
whether there's anyone left in town who are

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:08.370
still who they appear to be.

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:11.950
Jack Finney, the author of the novel on which
the film is based, claimed that there was

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:16.560
no political purpose behind his story at all,
but the film is nonetheless soaked in the

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.280
paranoia of its day.

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:21.190
Time Out's 2014 retrospective review explains:

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:26.580
"From fascism to communism to capitalist conformity
[...] this is the perfect distillation of

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:28.630
uncanny existential terror."

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:33.890
Critics thought little of Invasion of the
Body Snatchers in 1956, but, generations later,

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:36.170
it has risen to a Metascore of 92.

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:42.780
WALL-E is a visually stunning computer-animated
film with a heart of gold and something truly

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:44.460
worthwhile to say.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:49.070
The brainchild of director Andrew Stanton
and the celebrated Pixar animation studio,

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:54.450
WALL-E's titular character is one of Earth's
last "living" inhabitants, and has one programmed

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:58.500
purpose — to crush garbage into compact,
stackable cubes.

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:03.630
After years in isolation, however, he grows
beyond his programming, dreaming of romance

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:05.070
and adventure.

00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:09.470
WALL-E gets his wish when he discovers a living
plant among the ruins of our spoiled planet

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:14.140
and falls in love with EVE, the advanced probe
tasked with returning the plant to humanity's

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:15.710
new home in space.

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:20.110
Throughout their journey, the film dazzles
with beautiful animation and production design

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:25.440
and comments on modern consumerism and reliance
on technology, all the while remaining a romantic

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:26.770
comedy at heart.

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:29.680
WALL-E drew instant critical acclaim upon
its release.

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:31.510
New York Magazine's David Edelstein wrote:

00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:37.460
"The new Pixar picture WALL-E is one for the
ages, a masterpiece to be savored before or

00:19:37.460 --> 00:19:38.960
after the end of the world."

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:43.340
It was nominated for seven Academy Awards,
winning Best Animated Feature, and with a

00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:48.160
Metascore of 95, ranks in Metacritic's top
10 animated features of all time.

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:54.090
Director James Whale's Frankenstein shocked
new life into both horror and science fiction

00:19:54.090 --> 00:20:01.180
in 1931, but it's his sequel, 1935's The Bride
of Frankenstein, that stands as his true masterpiece;

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:05.810
it now holds a Metascore of 95, besting the
original by four points.

00:20:05.810 --> 00:20:10.840
In Bride of Frankenstein, Dr. Frankenstein's
mentor Dr. Pretorius coerces him into harvesting

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:14.650
more dead bodies with which to create a mate
for the lonely Creature.

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:15.920
"She's alive!"

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:20.460
Meanwhile, the Creature wanders the countryside
searching for meaning or companionship, only

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:24.910
to have it stolen from him at every opportunity
by closed-minded citizens.

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:29.630
The Bride of Frankenstein builds on the horror
of the first film while also adding a charming

00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:32.020
sense of self-awareness and humor.

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:36.680
Whale mines both scares and laughs from the
Creature's misadventures, and Dr. Pretorius

00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:38.430
is camp incarnate.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:42.740
The film is also full of moments and images
that have since become so iconic that some

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:47.090
may assume they originated in the previous
film, such as the Creature's grunts and broken

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:49.890
speech or his friendly encounter with the
blind hermit.

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:53.960
The Bride herself, with her statically charged
hairdo, is one of the most famous figures

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:57.380
of the genre despite appearing only briefly
in the film.

00:20:57.380 --> 00:21:01.090
Initially praised as merely "a first-rate
horror film," The Bride of Frankenstein is

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:03.700
now regarded as a true classic of the genre.

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:09.170
People want movies to take them places, and
that goes doubly for sci-fi movies.

00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:14.130
The 2013 movie Gravity takes its viewers to
the deadly and beautiful reaches of Earth's

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:18.900
orbit — and then holds them there, relentlessly,
for about 90 minutes.

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:23.650
The film stars Sandra Bullock as Dr. Ryan
Stone, an astronaut whose spacewalk is interrupted

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:26.960
when a sudden barrage of orbital debris destroys
her shuttle.

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:31.670
Alone in the vacuum of space, Dr. Stone must
find a way to rendezvous with another craft

00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:35.550
before her air runs out or the debris field
comes back around.

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:39.650
Director Alfonso Cuarón's took great pains
to keep the audience totally immersed in the

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:40.750
events of the film.

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:45.510
The story plays out in just about real-time,
and with as few cuts as possible.

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:50.161
In particular, Gravity begins with a 13-minute
continuous take that sets the tone for the

00:21:50.161 --> 00:21:55.040
entire film, in which the camera floats through
space and rarely leaves Ryan's side.

00:21:55.040 --> 00:22:00.590
The technological challenges of creating Gravity
meant it took over four years to complete,

00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:05.860
and the final product stands up as one of
the greatest examples of what IMAX 3D can

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:06.860
bring to a film.

00:22:06.860 --> 00:22:12.200
Much of what you see in the movie is computer-generated,
but Bullock's performance is very real, and

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:15.480
it even earned her an Academy Award nomination
for Best Actress.

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:20.410
The film would go on to win seven Oscars,
including Best Director for Cuarón, and maintains

00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:23.440
an astronomical Metascore of 96.

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:30.350
With a Metascore of 98, Metacritic's highest-scoring
science fiction film dates all the way back

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:34.220
to 1927, during the first golden age of cinema.

00:22:34.220 --> 00:22:38.900
The brainchild of German director Fritz Lang
and novelist Thea von Harbou, Metropolis is

00:22:38.900 --> 00:22:43.440
the story of a conflict between the suffering
workers and the pampered ruling class of a

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:45.130
massive futuristic city.

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:48.990
Much of the story centers around Freder, the
son of the city's "master" who joins with

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:50.840
the workers when he learns of their plight.

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:55.860
The star of the show, however, is Brigitte
Helm in the dual role of Maria, the kind-hearted

00:22:55.860 --> 00:23:00.950
leader of the workers, and the Maschinenmensch,
the wicked robot created in her image to incite

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:02.630
a war between the factions.

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:06.080
Metropolis wasn't exactly a critical smash
when it was first released, though.

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:08.640
New York Times film critic Mordaunt Hall wrote:

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:14.740
"It is a technical marvel with feet of clay,
a picture as soulless as the manufactured

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:16.050
woman of its story."

00:23:16.050 --> 00:23:20.340
But later generations were kinder to the film,
fixating on its revolutionary special effects

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:22.040
and unique production design.

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:26.600
Scarcity also contributed to the legacy of
Metropolis, as only abbreviated cuts were

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:29.060
available for most of the 20th century.

00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:36.124
Its 2003 restoration was celebrated by modern
film critics, however, leading to its near-perfect Metascore.

00:23:36.124 --> 00:23:38.140
